window.CS_NARR=window.CS_NARR||{};window.CS_NARR["hld"]={"hatlas": [[null, ["Hi, Buddy here. This sheet is about high level design, the interview where someone says \"design WhatsApp\" or \"design a URL shortener\", hands you a marker, and waits. It feels open ended, and that's exactly why people freeze. But here's the secret I want you to hold on to from the very first minute: system design interviews reward a repeatable method far more than they reward clever ideas. If you ask the right questions, put rough numbers on the board, draw a simple path, and then go deep on the two or three things that are genuinely hard about this particular problem, you will do well. Everything on this sheet supports that method.", "Let's start with the map, so you know where everything lives."]], ["hld-topic-atlas", ["The atlas splits high level design into eight areas, and I'd like you to think of them as layers of a building. At the bottom is the interview method: requirements, invariants, back of the envelope estimates, the latency and size numbers you should know by heart, and the design flow. That's the foundation, and it's the part most candidates skip.", "Next come the building blocks: DNS, CDNs, load balancers, how a request travels end to end, and the difference between batch processing, streaming and change data capture. These are the boxes you'll draw.", "Then data and storage, which is where designs usually succeed or fail: choosing a store by its access pattern, how indexes work inside, isolation levels and the anomalies they allow, replication, sharding, hot keys and caching.", "Then distributed systems theory: consistency models, CAP and PACELC, consensus and Raft, clocks and failure detection, distributed transactions and sagas, and conflict resolution with CRDTs. This is what senior interviewers dig into once your boxes are on the board.", "Then messaging and APIs: queues versus event logs, Kafka's partitions and consumer groups, and the protocols, REST, gRPC, WebSockets and server sent events.", "Then scale and resilience: rate limiting, timeouts, retries and circuit breakers, microservice patterns, deployments and migrations, multi region and disaster recovery, and the probabilistic data structures that make huge systems cheap.", "Then security and operations: OAuth, tokens, mutual TLS, the security checklist, observability and SLOs.", "And at the top, practice: thirty five problem breakdowns, eight fully worked designs, and the follow up questions interviewers love to ask."]], ["how-to-use-this-sheet", ["How should you use it? If you're learning from scratch, read the interview method first, then building blocks, then data and storage, in that order. Those three sections are the vocabulary for everything else; if you skip them, the later sections will feel like memorising trivia.", "Once that vocabulary feels comfortable, the distributed systems and resilience sections cover the senior level follow ups, the \"what happens if this node dies\" kind of questions.", "To practise, do this. Pick one of the worked designs, cover the page, set a forty five minute timer, and design it out loud, actually speaking, as if an interviewer were in the room. Then uncover the page and compare. The gaps you find are your study list. Speaking out loud matters, because the interview is a spoken performance, and thinking silently is a different skill.", "And if your interview is tomorrow, don't try to learn anything new. Review the numbers to know, the thirty five problem table, the follow up questions and the last minute checklist."]], ["the-five-questions-behind-every-design", ["Here's a framework that turns any design into five questions. If you can answer all five clearly, you've covered most of what interviewers score.", "One: what load must it handle, and what must never break? The second half is the important part. Every system has an invariant, a rule that must hold no matter what. A seat is sold at most once. Money is never created or lost. A message is never shown to the wrong person. Name the invariant early, because it decides where you need strong consistency.", "Two: where does the data live? Which store, what schema, what key.", "Three: how do reads stay fast? Usually caches, replicas, CDNs and precomputation.", "Four: how do writes stay correct? Transactions, isolation levels, idempotency, unique constraints.", "Five: what happens when parts fail? Timeouts, retries, failover, degradation, disaster recovery.", "Notice that the cheat sheet tells you where each question is covered. When you're practising, use these five as a checklist after each design."]]], "intro": [[null, ["Let's start with the method: how to run the hour, and how to get from a fuzzy requirement to an architecture you can defend."]], ["60-minute-interview-route", ["Here's a sixty minute route. Treat it as a budget, not a rigid script.", "The first five minutes are for the core use cases, the constraints and the invariant. Ask who the users are, which three to five actions matter, and what must never break. Write the answers down where the interviewer can see them.", "Minutes five to ten are for numbers: peak queries per second, storage, bandwidth and retention. Not every number, only the ones that will change a decision.", "Minutes ten to fifteen: the API and the core entities. A handful of endpoints and tables makes everything after it concrete.", "Minutes fifteen to thirty: the high level path. Client, then the edge, meaning the CDN, load balancer and gateway, then services, then stores. Keep this first diagram small. A clean diagram with six boxes beats a cluttered one with twenty.", "Minutes thirty to fifty are the heart of the interview: deep dives into two or three risks that are specific to this workload. For a URL shortener that's ID generation and redirect latency. For ticket booking it's double booking under a traffic spike. This is where you show depth.", "And the last ten minutes: failures, trade-offs, security and cost.", "Now, what are interviewers actually scoring? Five things. Requirements: do you ask before you draw, and do you write numbers down? Trade-offs: does every choice name its cost and the alternative you rejected? Bottlenecks: do you find the hot path, the hot key or the fan out before being asked? Failure thinking: do you cover retries, idempotency, degradation and data loss? And communication: do you drive the session, summarise, and check in at each step? That last one is underrated. Saying \"here's what I've got so far; shall I go deeper on storage or on the real time path?\" makes the interviewer feel like a collaborator.", "One final habit: save the last three to five minutes to walk your design back against the requirements you wrote at the start. It's a powerful way to close."]], ["design-flow", ["The design flow is a chain where each step feeds the next. Requirements and invariants lead to the workload and capacity. Capacity shapes the APIs and entities. Those give you the read and write paths. Then you look for the bottleneck, both for scale and for correctness. Then failure, security and recovery. And finally SLOs, cost and rollout.", "Why does order matter? Because each step uses the output of the previous one. If you skip requirements, your numbers are guesses. If your numbers are guesses, you can't justify sharding or caching. And then every later decision sounds arbitrary. A design that flows from its requirements sounds inevitable, and that's the feeling you want the interviewer to have."]], ["workload-signal-architecture-lever", ["This table is a shortcut from the shape of the workload to the right starting architecture. Learn to recognise the signal, and you'll know your first move.", "Read heavy, especially if it's global: start with a CDN, a cache, read replicas and precomputed results. Then watch for freshness, cache invalidation and hot keys. A news site or a product catalogue is like this.", "Write heavy, or when you need to replay history: start with a partitioned log, like Kafka, and asynchronous consumers. Watch ordering, back pressure and duplicates. Click tracking and metrics ingestion are like this.", "Long running jobs, like video transcoding or report generation: a queue, a pool of workers, and a status API the client can poll. Watch retry safety, dead letter queues and progress reporting.", "Real time updates, like chat or live scores: WebSockets, server sent events or a streaming log. Watch reconnects, fan out, and which server owns which connection.", "And media or search: object storage with a CDN, or a search index. Watch access control, freshness and search recall.", "When an interviewer describes a problem, say out loud which signal you see. \"This is extremely read heavy, so I'll lean on caching and a CDN.\" That one sentence tells them you know the playbook."]], ["clarifying-questions-checklist", ["Now the clarifying questions, and more importantly, why each one changes the design.", "Users and scope. Who are the users, which three to five actions matter, and what's out of scope? This prevents you building features nobody asked for, and it's the only row that's about what the system does. These are the functional requirements.", "Every row after that is a non functional requirement: how well the system must do its job.", "Scale: daily active users, peak queries per second, the read to write ratio, and data size today and in three to five years. This decides whether one database is enough or you need sharding, caching and asynchronous processing.", "Latency: the median and ninety ninth percentile targets for the main read and write paths. This decides caching, precomputation and whether you need regional deployments.", "Consistency: must users see their own writes immediately, and can others see stale data, and for how long? This decides strong versus eventual consistency, and you can decide it per operation.", "Availability: what's the SLO, single region or multi region, and what happens during an outage? This drives replication, failover and degradation.", "Durability: can any data be lost, and how long must it be kept? This drives synchronous replication, backups and storage tiers.", "Security and compliance: authentication, sensitive data like personal information or payments, and where data must physically live. This drives encryption, isolation and audit logs.", "Clients: web, mobile or third party? Offline support? Real time updates? This decides protocols and sync design.", "And cost and constraints: budget, team size, and existing systems that must be reused. This decides managed services versus self hosting.", "The interview tip at the bottom is gold: write the answers as a short list on the board, and refer back to them every time you justify a decision. \"We said p ninety nine under two hundred milliseconds, so the feed has to be precomputed.\""]]], "num": [[null, ["Numbers turn hand waving into engineering. You don't need many, but you need these few cold, so you can do the arithmetic while talking."]], ["latency-numbers", ["The latency ladder. The rungs climb by orders of magnitude, and that's the intuition to keep.", "An L1 cache hit is half a nanosecond. A branch mispredict is five nanoseconds. Main memory is about a hundred nanoseconds. Compressing a kilobyte takes a few microseconds. Sending two kilobytes over a gigabit network takes about twenty microseconds. Reading a megabyte sequentially from memory takes about a quarter of a millisecond. A round trip inside a data centre is half a millisecond. Reading a megabyte from an SSD is about a millisecond. A spinning disk seek is ten milliseconds, and reading a megabyte from that disk is twenty. And a round trip from California to the Netherlands and back is about a hundred and fifty milliseconds.", "What should you take away? Memory is roughly ten times faster than SSD for sequential reads, and about a million times faster than a cross continent round trip. So the expensive thing in modern systems is rarely computation. It's waiting: on disks, and above all on networks. That's why the standard advice is to avoid chatty cross region calls, batch requests, cache aggressively, and keep hot data in memory. If a design makes five sequential calls to another continent to serve one page, that's three quarters of a second gone before any work happens."]], ["storage-availability-sizes", ["More numbers. Spinning disks manage only one or two hundred random reads or writes per second. SATA SSDs manage around ninety thousand, and NVMe drives half a million to a million. That gap of thousands of times is why random access workloads moved to SSDs.", "Availability in nines. Three nines, ninety nine point nine percent, allows about eight point eight hours of downtime a year, or about forty four minutes a month. Four nines allows about fifty three minutes a year. Five nines allows about five minutes a year, which is barely enough time to notice an outage, let alone fix it by hand. So five nines means automated failover, no exceptions.", "Sizes: two to the ten is about a thousand, so kilobyte, megabyte, gigabyte and terabyte are each about a thousand times the last.", "Time: a day has eighty six thousand four hundred seconds, which I round to ten to the five. So a million requests a day is about twelve per second, and peaks are usually two to three times the average.", "And a Snowflake ID packs a forty one bit timestamp, ten bits of machine ID and a twelve bit sequence number into sixty four bits, which gives each machine four thousand ninety six unique IDs per millisecond, with no coordination at all."]], ["estimation-recipe", ["The estimation recipe. Queries per second equal daily active users times actions per user per day, divided by eighty six thousand four hundred. The peak is two to three times that, and for consumer flash events like a sale it can be ten times or more.", "Storage equals writes per day, times the size of each record, times how long you keep it, times the replication factor, usually three.", "Bandwidth is queries per second times payload size.", "A cache can usually hold about twenty percent of daily reads, by the eighty twenty rule, since a small fraction of items gets most traffic.", "And servers needed equal peak queries per second divided by what one server handles. A simple API server handles somewhere between one and ten thousand requests per second.", "Let's do the URL shortener example together. A hundred million new URLs a day, divided by ten to the five seconds, is about a thousand two hundred writes per second. With a hundred reads for every write, that's about a hundred and twenty thousand reads per second. Each row is about five hundred bytes, so a hundred million rows a day for five years is five hundred bytes times about a hundred and eighty billion rows, roughly ninety terabytes. And seven characters of base sixty two, meaning lowercase, uppercase and digits, give sixty two to the seventh power, about three and a half trillion codes, far more than we'll ever need.", "Some shortcuts worth memorising: a billion rows of a kilobyte each is about a terabyte. And one commodity server today has sixty four to two hundred and fifty six gigabytes of RAM and a few terabytes of SSD.", "And the most important line on this card: state only the numbers that change a design choice. If storage fits on one machine, say so in one sentence and move on. Interviewers don't reward arithmetic for its own sake; they reward arithmetic that drives a decision."]]], "to": [[null, ["This section is about trade-offs: the core choices, which store fits which access pattern, how caching works, and how architecture grows with scale. If system design had a single skill, it would be naming trade-offs well."]], ["core-trade-offs", ["Let's go through the core trade-offs, and notice that each one has a \"pick this when\" on both sides. There's no universally right answer, only answers right for a workload.", "During a network partition, do you reject conflicting writes, or accept them and reconcile later? Reject or limit them when an invariant needs strong consistency, like an account balance or a unique seat reservation. Accept them when stale or duplicate state is tolerable, like online presence or some feed signals.", "SQL or NoSQL? SQL when you need joins, transactions and ad hoc queries. NoSQL when you need huge scale with simple, known access patterns and a flexible schema.", "Synchronous or asynchronous? Synchronous when the user must wait for the result, like a login. Asynchronous for slow work, fan out or anything retryable, like sending emails.", "Push or pull? Push for users with few followers and real time needs. Pull for celebrities and inactive users. We'll see this in the news feed.", "Horizontal or vertical scaling? Horizontal for unbounded growth and high availability. Vertical when you're early and simplicity matters; a bigger machine is the cheapest scaling there is.", "Monolith or microservices? A monolith for a small team early on. Microservices when teams and scaling needs are genuinely independent. Many companies regret splitting too early.", "Stateful or stateless? Stateful where you must hold connections, like WebSocket gateways. Stateless everywhere else, like the API tier, so it can scale freely.", "Normalise or denormalise? Normalise for write heavy, consistency critical data. Denormalise for read heavy access where you want to avoid joins.", "And latency or throughput? Optimise latency for user facing paths, and throughput for batch and analytics.", "In the interview, the phrase to practise is: \"I'm choosing X because of requirement Y; the cost is Z; if Y changes, I'd switch to W.\" That one sentence shows you understand the trade-off rather than reciting a favourite tool."]], ["store-by-access-pattern", ["Choose a store by how the data is accessed, not by fashion. Relational data with transactions goes to Postgres or MySQL. Key lookups of hot data go to Redis or Memcached. Huge write volumes with wide rows go to Cassandra or DynamoDB. Full text search goes to Elasticsearch, which is built on an inverted index. Blobs like images, video and files go to object storage like S3, served through a CDN. Time series and metrics go to a time series database like Prometheus or InfluxDB. Graph relationships go to a graph database like Neo4j, or adjacency lists in a key value store if the traversals are shallow. Event streams go to Kafka. Geospatial queries use Redis GEO, geohash, H3 or S2 cells. And vectors for semantic search use an HNSW index, starting with pgvector inside Postgres, and moving to FAISS or a dedicated store only when you outgrow it.", "A good habit: when you add a store, say which access pattern justified it. \"Search needs relevance ranking and fuzzy matching, which Postgres does poorly at this scale, so I'm adding Elasticsearch fed by change data capture.\" Every extra store is something to operate, so each one has to earn its place."]], ["caching-strategies", ["Caching strategies describe how the cache and the database stay in step.", "Cache aside is the most common. The application asks the cache first; on a miss, it reads the database and puts the result in the cache. The cache only ever holds what's been read, which is efficient. The cost is that the first read after a change is slow, and you must invalidate on writes.", "Write through writes to the cache and database together. Reads are always warm and consistent, but every write is slower.", "Write back writes to the cache and flushes to the database later. Writes are very fast, but if the cache node dies before flushing, data is lost. That's acceptable for counters, not for orders.", "Write around writes only to the database, skipping the cache. That avoids filling the cache with data that won't be read soon, like bulk imports.", "Eviction decides what leaves when the cache is full: least recently used, least frequently used, or simply a time to live.", "Two problems to name. A stampede, where a popular key expires and many requests rush the database at once, is fixed with request coalescing, jittered expiry times and an origin shield. And a hot key, where one key gets enormous traffic, is fixed with a small in process cache plus splitting the key into several copies, like key hash one through key hash n, spread across nodes."]], ["architecture-by-scale", ["This card tells a story about growth. The insight is to know what breaks first at each stage, because that's how real systems evolve.", "At a thousand users, a single server and a single database are fine. Don't over engineer.", "At ten thousand users, database load breaks first, usually reads. Add read replicas and a cache.", "At a hundred thousand, the single application server becomes the bottleneck and a single point of failure. Add a load balancer, make the app tier stateless so you can run many copies, and put static assets on a CDN.", "At a million users, database writes break, because replicas only help reads. Now you shard, move slow work to asynchronous queues, and add a search index so search queries stop hammering the main database.", "At ten million and beyond, you go multi region, use CDNs everywhere, split users into cells, and lean on event driven design.", "In an interview, walking this ladder is far more convincing than jumping straight to the final architecture. It shows judgement: you add complexity only when a specific thing breaks."]], ["concept-problem-map", ["This map links concepts to the problems where they show up, and it's useful in reverse: when you hear a problem, recall its concept. Consistent hashing shows up in key value stores, distributed caches and GitHub's repository storage. Tries in autocomplete and typeahead. Inverted indexes in any search problem. Geohash and H3 in ride sharing, maps and \"find nearby\". Snowflake IDs in tweets and URL shorteners. Token buckets in rate limiters and API gateways. Min heaps and timer wheels in job schedulers. Fan out in Twitter, Instagram, notifications and chat. Distributed locks and set if not exists in Ticketmaster and booking. And Bloom filters in web crawlers and deduplication."]], ["reliability-toolkit", ["The reliability toolkit, in six lines, and these are the phrases to sprinkle through any design.", "Idempotency keys on every retryable write, so a retry can never double charge or double book.", "Retries with exponential backoff and jitter, circuit breakers and timeouts, so a failing dependency doesn't take you down with it.", "The outbox pattern to avoid dual writes, and sagas with compensations for multi step workflows. A dual write is when you write to the database and then publish to Kafka as two separate steps; if you crash in between, they disagree forever. The outbox fixes that.", "Leader election with Raft or ZooKeeper, and quorums, where reads plus writes exceed the number of replicas so they always overlap.", "Back pressure, dead letter queues, rate limits and graceful degradation, so overload slows things down instead of breaking them.", "And observability: metrics, logs and traces, with SLOs and error budgets, so you know when things are wrong before users tell you."]], ["caching-failure-modes", ["Caches fail in specific, named ways, and naming them impresses interviewers because it shows production experience.", "A stampede, also called a thundering herd: a hot key expires and thousands of requests hit the database at once. Fix it with request coalescing or a per key lock, so only one request rebuilds the value while others wait; refresh popular keys before they expire; or serve the stale value while revalidating in the background.", "Penetration: requests for keys that don't exist always miss and always hit the database. This is often an attack, someone requesting random IDs. Cache the \"not found\" result briefly, keep a Bloom filter of valid keys, and validate input.", "Avalanche: many keys expire at the same moment, or the cache cluster restarts cold, and the database is flooded. Add random jitter to expiry times so they spread out, warm the cache before sending traffic, and replicate the cache.", "Hot key: one key's traffic exceeds what a single cache node can serve, like a celebrity's profile. Use a local in process cache, split the key into copies, or put public data on the CDN.", "Big key: a huge value blocks the node and saturates the network. Split it into pages or fields, compress it, or store the blob elsewhere and cache only a pointer.", "Stale or inconsistent data: the cache and database disagree after concurrent writes. Delete the cache entry after the database commit, use short expiry times, version your keys, or drive invalidation from change data capture.", "And pollution: one off scans, like a nightly report, evict genuinely hot data. Use admission policies like TinyLFU, which only lets an item in if it's likely to be used again, and don't cache bulk reads."]]], "blocks": [[null, ["This section is a catalogue of every component you might draw: what it's for, the choice you have to make about it, and the number or failure mode worth saying out loud. When you draw a box, you should be able to say one sentence about why it's there and one about how it fails."]], ["components-at-a-glance", ["Let's walk through the components in the order a request meets them.", "DNS turns a name into an IP address, and it can do coarse routing, sending users to the nearest region or away from a failed one. The thing to say: DNS answers are cached according to their time to live, so DNS based failover takes minutes, not seconds. Keep TTLs low on records you might need to move.", "A CDN is a cache at the edge of the network, close to users, for static files, media and even some API responses. Choices include whether the CDN pulls from your origin on a miss or you push content to it, what goes into the cache key, signed URLs for private content, and an origin shield, a middle cache layer that protects your origin. The thing to say: a CDN typically offloads eighty to ninety five percent of static bytes, but invalidation is slow, so version your file names instead of invalidating.", "A load balancer spreads traffic across servers, checks their health and often terminates TLS. The big choice is layer four, which balances TCP connections and is very fast, versus layer seven, which understands HTTP and can route on paths and headers. The thing to say: the load balancer itself must be redundant, either an active passive pair or a managed service.", "An API gateway is the single front door: authentication, rate limiting, routing and sometimes aggregating responses. You might have one backend for each frontend, a mobile one and a web one. The thing to say: keep business logic out of the gateway, or it becomes a bottleneck every team has to change.", "The stateless app tier holds the business logic and scales horizontally. Stateless means no session data in the server's memory; sessions live in Redis or in a signed token. The thing to say: autoscale on CPU or on queue depth.", "A cache like Redis gives fast reads of hot data. Redis offers rich data structures and persistence; Memcached is simpler and multithreaded. The thing to say: one Redis node handles over a hundred thousand operations per second at sub millisecond latency.", "A relational database gives transactions, joins and constraints. Postgres or MySQL, with replicas and partitioning; Spanner or CockroachDB if you need globally distributed SQL. The thing to say: one primary handles roughly five to twenty thousand writes per second, depending on row size and indexes, which is more than many systems ever need.", "Key value and wide column stores, like DynamoDB, Cassandra and ScyllaDB, give massive write throughput with simple access. You design around a partition key and a clustering key. The thing to say: design one table per query, because there are no joins.", "Document databases like MongoDB store flexible JSON per entity. The thing to say: embed what's read together, and reference what grows without bound, like comments on a post.", "Search engines like Elasticsearch give full text search, facets and fuzzy matching, usually fed by change data capture from the main database. The thing to say: they're near real time, refreshing about every second, and never the source of truth.", "Object storage like S3 holds blobs: images, video, backups and data lakes. The thing to say: eleven nines of durability, and clients should upload directly using pre signed URLs, so large files never pass through your servers.", "A message queue like SQS or RabbitMQ distributes tasks. Key concepts are the visibility timeout, how long a message is hidden while a worker processes it, and the dead letter queue. The thing to say: delivery is at least once, so consumers must be idempotent.", "An event log like Kafka is an ordered, replayable stream. The thing to say: ordering is guaranteed only within a partition.", "A stream processor like Flink does stateful real time aggregation using windows, watermarks and checkpoints.", "A warehouse or lakehouse does analytics over history, using columnar formats like Parquet so scans read only the columns they need.", "Coordination services like ZooKeeper and etcd handle leader election, configuration and locks. The thing to say: they're small and strongly consistent, so never store application data in them.", "A workflow engine like Temporal or Step Functions makes multi step processes durable, so retries and timers survive crashes.", "And service discovery or a service mesh helps services find each other, adding mutual TLS, retries and telemetry. The thing to say: a mesh adds a network hop, but centralises policy."]], ["load-balancing-algorithms", ["Load balancing algorithms, and when each fits. Round robin, or weighted round robin, works when requests are uniform and short. Least connections, or least outstanding requests, works when request durations vary, because a server stuck on slow requests gets fewer new ones. Power of two choices is beautifully simple: pick two servers at random and send the request to the less loaded one. With almost no shared state, it gets close to optimal on large fleets, which is why it's popular. Hashing by IP, or consistent hashing, gives session affinity and cache locality, sending the same user or key to the same server. And latency aware balancing, using a moving average of response times, suits backends with different hardware."]], ["data-processing-styles", ["Data processing styles. OLTP, online transaction processing, uses row stores for lots of small reads and writes. OLAP, online analytical processing, uses column stores for big scans and aggregations. ETL transforms data before loading it into the warehouse; ELT loads raw data first and transforms it inside the warehouse, often with a tool like dbt, which is the modern default.", "The lambda architecture runs two paths: a batch layer that's slow but correct, and a speed layer that's fast but approximate, merged at query time. Its cost is maintaining two codebases that must agree. The kappa architecture uses one streaming path, and when you need to recompute, you replay the log. Simpler, if your log keeps enough history.", "Change data capture, with a tool like Debezium, reads the database's own transaction log, like the Postgres write ahead log or the MySQL binlog, and streams every change into Kafka, from where it feeds search indexes, caches and the warehouse. It's the cleanest way to keep derived systems in sync, because the application doesn't need to remember to publish anything.", "File formats: Parquet and ORC are columnar, ideal for analytics scans. Avro is row based and handles schema evolution well, ideal for messages."]], ["little-s-law-and-utilisation", ["Little's law is one of the most useful formulas in system design: the number of requests in flight equals the arrival rate times the time each one takes. A thousand requests per second, each taking two hundred milliseconds, means two hundred requests in flight at any moment. That directly sizes your thread pools and connection pools. If your database pool only has fifty connections, you have a problem.", "Second, queueing delay. Waiting time doesn't grow linearly with load; it grows sharply as utilisation passes seventy or eighty percent, and explodes near a hundred. That's why you plan headroom rather than running servers hot.", "Third, tail latency under fan out. If one request calls a hundred backend services in parallel, and each is slow just one percent of the time, then about sixty three percent of requests hit at least one slow call, because one minus point nine nine to the hundredth power is about point six three. So in microservice systems, the tail of each service becomes the typical experience of the user. That's why techniques like hedged requests and per hop timeouts matter."]], ["request-path-through-a-typical-web-system", ["This diagram shows a typical request path, and it's worth being able to draw from memory. Static content and media go to the CDN edge, and only misses go back to the origin. Dynamic requests hit the load balancer, then the API gateway for authentication and rate limiting, then stateless services. The services read from the cache first, and on a miss from the database, with read replicas taking read traffic. Writes go to the primary database, and the service also puts an event on a queue or log. Asynchronous workers consume those events to update object storage, the search index, notifications and analytics.", "So the read path is CDN, then cache, then replica. And the write path is the primary database plus an event, with everything else derived asynchronously. That split is the backbone of most designs."]], ["proxy-gateway-load-balancer-mesh-who-does-what", ["These components overlap, which confuses people, so the trick is to name the job, not the product.", "A forward proxy sits in front of clients, filtering and caching their outbound traffic, like a corporate proxy. A reverse proxy sits in front of servers, terminating TLS, compressing, caching and hiding the backends; Nginx and Envoy are examples. A load balancer sits in front of a pool of servers, spreading traffic, checking health and draining connections during deploys. An API gateway sits at the edge of your services, handling authentication, rate limits, routing, request transformation, API keys and usage plans. And a service mesh sits beside every service as a sidecar, handling service to service mutual TLS, retries, timeouts, traffic shifting and telemetry.", "In practice, one product often plays several roles; Envoy can be a reverse proxy, a load balancer and a mesh sidecar. So in an interview, say \"a layer seven load balancer that also terminates TLS\" rather than just \"Nginx\"."]]], "db": [[null, ["Data store choices are where designs usually succeed or fail. This section goes inside the databases, so you can justify a choice and answer \"how does it scale\" and \"what anomalies can happen\"."]], ["index-and-storage-engines", ["Storage engines and indexes. There are two big families of storage engine, and the difference explains a lot.", "B plus trees keep data in sorted pages on disk and update them in place. They're excellent at point lookups and range scans, which is why Postgres and MySQL use them. The cost is random writes, and pages splitting as they fill.", "LSM trees, log structured merge trees, take a completely different approach. Writes go into an in memory table and an append only log. When the memory table fills, it's flushed to disk as a sorted, immutable file. Background compaction merges these files over time. Because all writes are sequential, throughput is very high, which is why Cassandra, RocksDB and LevelDB use LSM trees. The cost is that a read may have to check several files, which is read amplification, and space is used by old versions until compaction, which is space amplification. Bloom filters help reads skip files that can't contain the key.", "Other indexes: hash indexes give constant time point lookups but no ranges. Inverted indexes map each word to the documents containing it, powering full text search, with some lag while the index updates. Geo indexes like geohash, quadtrees, S2, H3 and R trees answer \"what's nearby\". Vector indexes like HNSW and IVF answer \"what's most similar\", trading recall against memory and latency. And bitmap or columnar indexes speed up analytics filters but make single row updates slow.", "Two practical rules for composite indexes. The leftmost prefix rule: an index on columns A, B, C can serve queries on A, or A and B, or all three, but not on B alone. So put equality columns first and the range column last. And a covering index, one that includes every column a query needs, avoids looking up the table at all."]], ["isolation-levels-and-anomalies", ["Isolation levels describe what concurrent transactions can see of each other. Weaker levels are faster but allow anomalies.", "Read uncommitted allows dirty reads, seeing another transaction's changes before it commits. Almost nobody uses it.", "Read committed, the Postgres default, prevents dirty reads, but allows non repeatable reads, where reading the same row twice gives different answers, and lost updates, where two transactions read, modify and write the same row and one change silently disappears.", "Repeatable read, or snapshot isolation, MySQL's default, gives each transaction a consistent snapshot, so repeated reads agree. But it still allows write skew.", "Serializable, implemented with two phase locking or serializable snapshot isolation, prevents all of these, at the cost of throughput and of transactions being aborted and retried on conflict.", "Two anomalies to know cold. The lost update: two people decrement the same stock count; both read ten, both write nine, and one sale vanishes. Fix it with select for update, which locks the row; with an atomic update like \"set count to count minus one where count is greater than zero\"; or with an optimistic version column.", "Write skew is subtler, and the classic example is two doctors on call. The rule is that at least one doctor must be on call. Both doctors check, see that the other is on call, and both go off call at the same time. Each transaction was individually valid, but together they broke the rule, and snapshot isolation doesn't catch it because they wrote different rows. You need serializable isolation, or an explicit lock or constraint on the shared condition.", "Finally, ACID versus BASE: atomicity, consistency, isolation and durability, versus basically available, soft state and eventually consistent. Two philosophies for two kinds of data."]], ["replication", ["Replication modes, each with its trade-off.", "Single leader: all writes go to one node, which streams changes to followers. Simple, with no write conflicts. The costs: the leader is the write bottleneck, and failover is risky, because promoting a follower that's behind can lose writes.", "Multi leader: several nodes accept writes, useful for multiple regions or offline clients, like a calendar app on your phone. The cost is conflicts, resolved by last write wins, CRDTs or application logic.", "Leaderless, the Dynamo style used by Cassandra: clients write to several replicas and read from several, with tunable quorums. Highly available. Repairs happen through read repair, where a read notices a stale replica and fixes it, and hinted handoff, where a node holds writes for a temporarily dead neighbour.", "Synchronous replication waits for followers, so no data is lost on failover, but every write pays the latency, and one slow replica blocks everything. Asynchronous replication is fast, but followers lag, and a failover can lose the most recent writes.", "Replication lag causes real user visible bugs, so know the three fixes. Read your own writes: after a user changes their profile, read their own data from the leader, or wait until the replica has caught up. Monotonic reads: pin a user to one replica, so they never see time go backwards by hitting a staler replica on the next request. And consistent prefix: keep causally related writes in the same partition, so nobody sees an answer before its question."]], ["partitioning-sharding", ["Sharding splits data across machines. The strategy depends on your queries.", "Range partitioning, by key ranges like dates or alphabetical ranges, is great for range scans and time series, but the newest partition gets all the writes and becomes hot.", "Hash partitioning spreads load evenly, but you lose range queries, and changing the number of shards moves most of the data, if you use simple modulo hashing.", "Consistent hashing with virtual nodes fixes that: nodes sit at many points around a ring, and a key belongs to the next node clockwise. When a node joins or leaves, only about one over N of the keys move. That's ideal for elastic clusters.", "Directory based placement keeps a lookup table of which shard holds what. Very flexible, you can move a big tenant to its own shard, but the directory is on the critical path of every request, so it must be fast and highly available.", "Geo or tenant partitioning supports data residency and isolation, but tenants are rarely equal in size.", "For hot keys, add a random suffix, like key hash zero through key hash n, to spread one logical key across shards, and merge on read; or cache it locally.", "Secondary indexes on sharded data come in two flavours. Local indexes live inside each shard, so a query by the secondary attribute must ask every shard, a scatter gather. Global indexes are partitioned by the indexed value itself, so reads are targeted, but they're updated asynchronously.", "And the golden rule: choose the shard key so that most transactions touch only one shard, because cross shard transactions are slow and complex. For a chat app, shard by conversation. For a multi tenant SaaS, shard by tenant."]], ["picking-a-data-model", ["Picking a data model, as a series of questions. Do you need multi row transactions or strong constraints, like money or inventory? Use a relational database, and keep the invariant inside one database. Is access always by a known key at huge scale? Use key value or wide column, modelling one table per query. Is each entity a self contained, evolving document, like a product with varying attributes? A document store. Do queries traverse relationships many hops deep, like \"friends of friends who like this\"? A graph database, or adjacency lists plus a cache if the traversals are shallow. Append only measurements over time? A time series database with downsampling and retention. Free text relevance or facets? A search index fed by change data capture from the source of truth. Semantic similarity? A vector index, starting with pgvector and moving to a dedicated store at around ten million vectors or more.", "Often the answer is several of these together, a relational source of truth with a search index and cache derived from it. That's normal; just make clear which one is authoritative."]], ["cache-aside-read-and-write", ["This sequence diagram shows cache aside in detail. On a read, the app asks the cache for the key. On a miss, it selects the row from the database, then sets the key in the cache with a time to live. The next read is a hit.", "The subtle part is the note about writes: update the database first, then delete the cache key, rather than writing the new value into the cache. Why delete instead of set? Imagine two concurrent writers. If each sets the cache after writing the database, they can finish in a different order than they committed, leaving the old value in the cache indefinitely. Deleting means the next read loads whatever the database actually holds. There's still a tiny race window, which is why short TTLs remain a good safety net."]]], "hld": [[null, ["This section is a big table of thirty five classic problems. For each one, it lists the three deep dives interviewers usually push on, and the one insight that unlocks the design. I'll walk through them in groups, and for each I'll explain why the insight matters, because the \"why\" is what lets you reuse it on problems you've never seen. Feel free to skip ahead with the next button whenever a group feels familiar."]], ["every-hld-problem-3-deep-dives-the-one-insight", ["Let's start with the simpler, classic problems.", "URL shortener. The deep dives are ID generation, redirect latency and custom aliases. The insight: it's about a hundred reads for every write, so it's a caching problem. Keep hot codes in Redis, and serve popular redirects from the CDN. And use a three oh two redirect rather than a three oh one if the business wants analytics. A three oh one is a permanent redirect that browsers cache, so repeat clicks never reach you and you can't count them. A three oh two is temporary, so every click comes through.", "Pastebin. The deep dives are large content, expiry and abuse. The insight is separating metadata from content: the small record, with its ID, owner and expiry, goes in a database, while the paste itself goes in object storage and is served through a CDN. Databases are bad at storing large blobs; object stores are built for it.", "Rate limiter. The deep dives are the algorithm, making it distributed, and hot rules. The insight: a token bucket in Redis, with the check and the decrement done in one Lua script so they're atomic. Without atomicity, two servers can both see one token left and both allow a request. And decide what happens when Redis is down; usually you fail open, letting traffic through, because blocking all users is worse than briefly allowing a few extra requests.", "Unique ID generator. The deep dives are Snowflake, the alternatives, and clock sync. The insight: a Snowflake style ID packs a timestamp, a machine ID and a sequence number into sixty four bits. Machines never need to talk to each other, and the IDs sort roughly by time, which makes them great database keys. The danger is a clock going backwards, so the generator must refuse to issue IDs until the clock catches up.", "Key value store. The deep dives are consistent hashing, vector clocks and failure detection. The insight is the write path: append to a commit log for durability, put the write in an in memory table, and flush to sorted files on disk, the LSM tree we discussed. Spread keys with consistent hashing and use quorum reads and writes for tunable consistency.", "Leaderboard. The deep dives are sorted set internals, segmenting by time or region, and percentiles. The insight: Redis sorted sets. Adding a score and asking for a player's rank are both logarithmic, because sorted sets are built on skip lists. A database query that sorts millions of rows on every request can't compete.", "Autocomplete. The deep dives are trie storage, trending terms and personalisation. The insight is that most traffic is for short prefixes, so precompute the top suggestions for each short prefix and cache them in Redis or at the CDN. A full trie is only needed for the long tail of rare prefixes.", "Booking. The deep dives are double booking, availability search and refunds. The insight: hold, then confirm. A hold reserves the item for a few minutes with an expiry; confirming after payment makes it permanent. Idempotency keys make the payment step safe to retry.", "Web crawler. The deep dives are politeness, deduplication and recrawl priority. The insight: a priority queue of URLs, called the frontier, plus a Bloom filter to remember billions of seen URLs in little memory, plus per domain rate limits so you don't hammer any one website.", "E-commerce. The deep dives are overselling, catalogue search and order processing. The insight for flash sales: decrement stock atomically in Redis, which can handle the spike, and let the database catch up asynchronously. The Redis counter is the gate; the database is the record."]], ["every-hld-problem-3-deep-dives-the-one-insight", ["Now the feed, messaging and media problems.", "Instagram. The deep dives are push versus pull feeds, media upload and the social graph. The insight is hybrid fan out: push new posts into followers' feeds for normal users, but pull celebrity posts at read time. Otherwise one celebrity post means writing to a hundred million feeds.", "Twitter feed. The same celebrity problem, plus sharding and trending topics. The insight here is about interview behaviour: raise the hotspot problem yourself, before the interviewer does. It's the obvious follow up, and getting there first shows you think ahead.", "Notification system. The deep dives are at least once delivery, preferences and quiet hours, and device tokens. The insight: check preferences and deduplicate before you fan out. It's far cheaper to drop a notification early than to send it to five channels and then discover the user opted out.", "Typeahead. The deep dives are tries versus Elasticsearch, ranking and freshness. The insight: compute the top suggestions offline, serve common prefixes from the CDN, and blend in trending terms from a Kafka stream. Most of the work happens before anyone types.", "WhatsApp. The deep dives are WebSocket routing, ordering and exactly once delivery, and end to end encryption. The insight: a registry in Redis mapping each user to the server holding their connection. Any server can then find and forward a message to any user.", "YouTube. The deep dives are transcoding, adaptive bitrate streaming and view counts. The insight: split the upload path, which is slow and asynchronous, from the playback path, which is fast and served by CDNs. They have completely different requirements, so they shouldn't share infrastructure.", "Slack. The deep dives are fan out to large channels, ordering and message search. The insight is the same as WhatsApp's: the map from user to gateway is the core of the design. Everything else builds on knowing where each user is connected."]], ["every-hld-problem-3-deep-dives-the-one-insight", ["Next, the infrastructure problems, where you're designing the building blocks themselves.", "Distributed cache. The deep dives are the hash ring, replication and eviction. The insight: use virtual nodes so load spreads evenly and rebalancing is smooth, and handle hot keys with a local cache plus splitting the key across nodes.", "Job scheduler. The deep dives are duplicate execution, scaling to ten million jobs, and retries. The insight: workers claim a job with a compare and swap on its status, changing it from pending to running only if it's still pending, so exactly one worker wins. And make jobs idempotent, because a worker can crash after doing the work but before marking it done.", "Message queue. The deep dives are the partitioned log, consumer groups and exactly once semantics. The insight is Kafka's design: partitioned append only logs, consumers that pull at their own pace, and replication to a set of in sync replicas for durability.", "Dropbox or Google Drive. The deep dives are chunking, conflicts and deduplication. The insight: split files into chunks and name each chunk by the SHA two fifty six hash of its content. Identical chunks across all users are stored once, and when you edit a file, only the changed chunks are uploaded. That's content addressing, and it's also how Git works.", "GitHub. The deep dives are Git storage, code search and merges. The insight: route each repository to a replica set of storage servers using consistent hashing, so a repository's data lives together and can be replicated as a unit.", "Google Maps. The deep dives are routing, live traffic and arrival time estimates. The insight: contraction hierarchies, a preprocessing technique that adds shortcut edges to the road graph, so a cross country route takes milliseconds instead of seconds of plain Dijkstra."]], ["every-hld-problem-3-deep-dives-the-one-insight", ["Now booking, location and money problems, where correctness is the whole game.", "Hotel booking. The deep dives are double booking, search and dynamic pricing. The insight: a ten minute hold with an expiry, plus a unique constraint on room and date in the database as the final safety net. Even if application logic has a bug, the database refuses a second booking of the same room on the same night.", "Ticketmaster. The deep dives are seat locking, flash sales and waitlists. The insight: Redis set if not exists with a ten minute expiry to lock seats quickly, plus a virtual waiting room that admits users at a controlled rate. The waiting room turns an uncontrollable spike into a steady stream.", "Ride sharing. The deep dives are the geo index, matching and surge pricing. The insight: index drivers by H3 or S2 cell in Redis, and search only the rider's cell and its neighbours. Computing the distance to every driver on every request is a full scan that won't scale.", "Payments. The deep dives are idempotency, the ledger and PCI compliance. The insight: idempotency keys, a double entry ledger, and the rule that a timeout is not a failure. If the payment provider times out, the charge may have succeeded, so you find out before retrying or refunding.", "Stock exchange. The deep dives are the order book, price time matching and recovery. The insight surprises people: a single threaded matching engine per stock symbol. Matching must be strictly ordered, and one thread with everything in memory is both simplest and fastest, handling millions of orders per second without locks. You scale by symbol, not by adding threads.", "Ad click aggregator. The deep dives are deduplication, aggregation in Flink, and fraud. The insight: a lambda architecture, with Flink giving real time counts for dashboards and a Spark batch job producing the exact counts used for billing. Advertisers pay on the batch numbers, which can be recomputed and audited."]], ["every-hld-problem-3-deep-dives-the-one-insight", ["And the final group: platforms and modern AI systems.", "Metrics and monitoring. The deep dives are time series compression, queries and alerting. The insight: control cardinality at ingestion. Every unique combination of labels is a separate time series, so one careless label, like a user ID, can create millions of series and take the system down.", "CDN. The deep dives are anycast routing, cache tiers and invalidation. The insight: anycast, where every edge location announces the same IP address so the network routes each user to the nearest one, plus an origin shield, a middle tier that collapses misses from many edges into one request to your origin.", "Search engine. The deep dives are the inverted index, PageRank and ranking. The insight: an inverted index plus BM25 scoring is the core of every search system, and everything else, like link analysis and learned ranking, is layered on top.", "LLM chat. The deep dives are streaming, the context window and GPU routing. The insight: stream tokens with server sent events so the user sees the answer forming, and route follow up messages to the GPU that already holds that conversation's KV cache, which avoids recomputing the whole prompt.", "RAG, retrieval augmented generation. The deep dives are chunking, the HNSW index, and hybrid search with reranking. The insight is a division of labour: approximate nearest neighbour search for recall, finding candidates by meaning; a cross encoder for precision, carefully reranking the top few; and BM25 for exact terms like product codes and names, which embeddings often miss.", "And gaming leaderboards. The deep dives are sorted sets, friends leaderboards and tournaments. The insight: don't maintain a separate sorted set for every user's friends. Instead, look up each friend's score on demand from the global set, which is fast because a typical friend list is small.", "That's all thirty five. If you notice the same few ideas repeating, caching, fan out, idempotency, atomic claims, geo cells and splitting slow paths from fast ones, that's exactly the point. There are only a few dozen real ideas in system design, recombined."]], ["example-whatsapp-message-flow", ["This diagram makes the WhatsApp flow concrete. The sender's phone holds a WebSocket connection to gateway one. The message goes to the message service, which stores it in Cassandra, partitioned by conversation, and looks up in Redis which gateway holds the receiver's connection. Here it's gateway two, so the message is forwarded there and pushed down the receiver's WebSocket. If the receiver is offline, there's no gateway entry, so a push notification goes out instead, and the message waits in storage until the app reconnects and syncs."]], ["example-hybrid-feed-fan-out", ["And this diagram shows hybrid fan out. When someone posts, the system checks how many followers the author has. For a normal user, it fans out on write, pushing the post ID into each follower's precomputed timeline. For a celebrity, it stores the post only in the celebrity's own timeline. When a reader opens their feed, the service takes their precomputed timeline and merges in recent posts from any celebrities they follow. Readers get fast feeds, and nobody pays the cost of writing one post into a hundred million timelines."]]], "worked": [[null, ["Now the worked designs. Each one runs the full sixty minute route: requirements, estimates, API and data model, architecture, deep dives and trade-offs. Treat them as model answers. The best way to use them is to attempt each problem out loud first, and only then listen to how I'd do it, noticing where your version differed."]], ["url-shortener", ["Let's design a URL shortener. It's a read heavy key lookup, and the interesting parts are generating short unique codes and keeping redirects under about fifty milliseconds at more than a hundred thousand reads per second.", "Requirements: shorten a long URL to a seven character code, redirect from the code, support optional custom aliases and expiry, and record click analytics. A hundred million new URLs a day, a hundred reads per write, a ninety ninth percentile redirect time under fifty milliseconds, and high availability, because a broken short link is a broken link everywhere it was shared.", "Estimates: about twelve hundred writes per second, peaking near three thousand; about a hundred and twenty thousand reads per second, peaking near three hundred and fifty thousand; and about ninety terabytes over five years. Seven characters of base sixty two give three and a half trillion codes.", "API: a post to create a short URL, taking the long URL and optional alias and expiry, returning the code. And a get on the code, returning a three oh two redirect to the long URL. The table is simple: code as the primary key, the long URL, the owner, and created and expiry times. Click events go onto Kafka and into an analytics store.", "Now the deep dives. First, code generation. The naive approach is to hash the long URL and take seven characters, but then you must check for collisions on every write. A cleaner approach: a coordinator hands each server a range of a million IDs at a time. The server counts through its range, encodes each number in base sixty two, and scrambles it with a reversible mapping so codes aren't sequential and guessable. Because every number is unique, there are no collisions and no checks. If a server dies, the unused part of its range is simply wasted, which is fine with trillions available.", "Second, the read path. Popular redirects are cached briefly at the CDN. Behind that, Redis holds hot codes with least recently used eviction. Behind that, the key value store is partitioned by a hash of the code. Most reads never reach the database.", "Third, custom aliases. Use a conditional insert, insert only if the code doesn't exist, which gives uniqueness without locks.", "And expiry: store the expiry time, check it on read, and clean up in the background.", "Trade-offs to mention: a three oh one redirect is cached by browsers, saving load but losing analytics, while a three oh two keeps every click visible. Hashing the URL avoids a coordinator but costs collision checks. And abuse: scan destination URLs for malware and rate limit creators, because shorteners are popular with spammers."]], ["news-feed-home-timeline", ["The news feed is about fan out on write versus fan out on read, and the celebrity problem decides the design, so raise it yourself, early.", "Requirements: post text and media, follow users, and see a ranked or chronological home feed with infinite scroll. Three hundred million daily users, feed loads under two hundred milliseconds at the ninety ninth percentile, and eventual consistency is fine; nobody minds if a post shows up a few seconds late.", "Estimates: three hundred million users posting twice a day is about seven thousand posts per second. Reading the feed ten times a day is about thirty five thousand feed reads per second, peaking near a hundred thousand. If each post fans out to two hundred followers, that's about one point four million cache writes per second. And caching eight hundred post IDs per user, at eight bytes each, for three hundred million users, is about two terabytes, which fits comfortably in a Redis cluster.", "API: post to create a post, get the feed with a cursor, and post to follow someone. Posts have Snowflake IDs, so they sort by time. The follows table has an index in both directions, who I follow and who follows me. And each user's feed is a Redis list of recent post IDs.", "The deep dives. First, hybrid fan out. When a normal user posts, fan out workers read their followers from the social graph and push the post ID into each follower's Redis list. Reads are then a single fast list read. But for authors with more than about ten thousand followers, pushing would mean millions of writes per post, so instead their posts are pulled at read time and merged in.", "Second, ranking. Candidates come from the cached feed plus the celebrity pull. Then you compute features, score them with a lightweight model, and apply diversity rules so one author doesn't dominate.", "Third, inactive users. Skip fan out for users who haven't logged in for weeks, and rebuild their feed when they return. That saves a huge amount of wasted writes.", "And media goes to object storage through pre signed URLs and is served by the CDN.", "Trade-offs: push gives fast reads but amplifies writes; pull gives cheap writes but slow reads; hybrid takes the best of both. Deletes and privacy changes need invalidation, often with tombstones. And use cursor pagination on the post ID, not page numbers, so pages stay stable as new posts arrive at the top."]], ["chat-messaging-whatsapp-style", ["Chat is about long lived connections, routing each message to whichever gateway holds the recipient, ordering within a conversation, and delivering to people who are offline.", "Requirements: one to one and group chats up to five hundred members, sent, delivered and read receipts, presence, offline delivery and sync across devices. Five hundred million daily users sending forty messages a day, delivered in under half a second when online.", "Estimates: that's about two hundred and thirty thousand messages per second, peaking near seven hundred thousand. At a hundred bytes each, about two terabytes a day. And about a hundred million open connections; at fifty to a hundred thousand connections per gateway server, that's one to two thousand gateways.", "API: messages travel over the WebSocket. The client sends the conversation ID, a client generated message ID and the body, and the server acknowledges with a sequence number. Receipts flow back the same way. History is fetched with a normal HTTP get, paging backwards by sequence number. Messages are stored in Cassandra, partitioned by conversation and clustered by sequence, so loading a conversation's history is one sequential read. Presence lives in Redis with a short expiry.", "The deep dives. Routing: when a client connects, its gateway registers \"this user is on me\" in Redis. To deliver a message, the message service looks up the recipient's gateway and forwards it there.", "Ordering: each conversation gets sequence numbers from the shard that owns it, so there's one authority per conversation. Clients sort by sequence and use the client message ID to drop duplicates.", "Delivery: at least once, with idempotent writes. If an acknowledgement is lost, the client resends with the same client message ID, and the server recognises it.", "Groups: store each message once, and fan it out only to members who are online; very large groups switch to a pull model.", "Security: end to end encryption with the Signal protocol, so the server only ever stores ciphertext.", "Trade-offs: keeping history on the server enables multi device sync but costs storage and privacy, while deleting after delivery is the opposite. If a gateway crashes, clients reconnect to another and resync from their last acknowledged sequence number. And presence is surprisingly expensive, so publish it only to contacts who are actually looking at the chat."]], ["ticket-booking-with-flash-sales", ["Ticket booking has one invariant: a seat is sold at most once. Everything else, the holds, the payments, the waiting room, exists to protect that invariant during a traffic spike.", "Requirements: browse events, view the seat map, hold seats for ten minutes, pay, and get a confirmed ticket. No double booking, while surviving a million users arriving for fifty thousand seats.", "Estimates: normally around a thousand bookings per second. During a flash sale, a million people are waiting, and you admit two to five thousand per second. Seat map reads vastly outnumber writes, so cache them with a short expiry.", "API: get the seat map, post a hold with the seat IDs, returning a hold ID and expiry, and post a booking with the hold ID and a payment token, plus an idempotency key header. The seats table has the event, seat, status, hold ID, hold expiry and a version number.", "The deep dives. The atomic hold is one conditional update: set these seats to held, with this hold ID, only where they're currently available. Then check that the number of rows changed equals the number of seats requested. If someone else grabbed one seat in the meantime, the counts won't match, and you roll back. Redis set if not exists can sit in front to absorb load, but the database remains the source of truth.", "Expiry: every hold carries a deadline. A sweeper releases expired holds, and reads treat an expired hold as available even before the sweeper runs, so seats never get stuck.", "Payment: use an idempotency key, and confirm when the payment provider's webhook arrives. A timeout is unknown, not failed, so reconcile with the provider before releasing anyone's seats.", "The flash sale: a virtual waiting room issues signed tokens at a fixed admission rate, so the booking system sees a steady stream instead of a wall. Pre scale before the sale opens, and serve the seat map from cache.", "Trade-offs: optimistic locking, with a version check, scales better when contention is spread out, while pessimistic row locks are simpler when many people want the same seats. Partition seats by event, so each event's state lives on one primary. And for very hot events, decide on fairness: strict queue order or a lottery."]], ["ride-hailing", ["Ride hailing combines three challenges: a firehose of location writes, a geo index for \"who is near\", and an atomic match so one driver is never offered two rides.", "Requirements: request a ride, match a nearby driver within seconds, live tracking, a fare estimate with surge pricing, and the trip lifecycle. Five million active drivers, and matching within five seconds at the ninety ninth percentile.", "Estimates: five million drivers sending a location every four seconds is one and a quarter million writes per second. That sounds scary, but keeping only the latest point per driver, about fifty bytes, is only two hundred and fifty megabytes, which fits in memory easily. Ride requests are only one to five thousand per second.", "API: drivers stream location over a WebSocket. Riders post a ride request and get back a ride ID, estimated arrival and fare, and follow the ride live with server sent events or a WebSocket. The geo index maps each cell to the set of drivers in it.", "The deep dives. The geo index: divide the map into H3 hexagons or geohash cells, kept in memory and sharded by city. To find nearby drivers, search the pickup cell and the rings of cells around it.", "Matching: rank candidates by estimated arrival time on the actual road network, not straight line distance, because a driver across a river may be close as the crow flies but far by road. Reserve the chosen driver atomically before sending the offer, give them a few seconds to accept, and move to the next candidate if they don't.", "Write volume: only the latest point goes into the index. The full location history streams to Kafka for analytics and for training the arrival time models.", "Surge: compute the ratio of demand to supply per cell per minute from the stream.", "Trade-offs: more frequent updates mean better accuracy but more battery and write load, so adapt the frequency to speed and trip state. Sharding by city keeps queries local but complicates trips across city boundaries. And notice the asymmetry: trip state changes need transactions, but location data can be lossy, since a missed update is replaced four seconds later."]], ["payment-system", ["In a payment system, money must never be created, lost or charged twice. Three ideas carry the design: idempotency, a double entry ledger and reconciliation.", "Requirements: charge, refund and pay out; support retries safely; show payment status; and never double charge. Thousands of payments per second at peak, strong consistency for balances, and a full audit trail.", "The payment is a state machine: created, then authorised, then captured, then settled, with branches to failed and refunded. And the most important rule on the card: a timeout from the payment provider means the state is unknown. You query the provider. You never assume it failed, because if it actually succeeded and you charge again, the customer pays twice.", "API: post a payment with an idempotency key header, the order ID, the amount in minor units like paise or cents, the currency and a card token. Amounts are integers in minor units because floating point can't represent money exactly. Tables: payments with a status and version; idempotency records with the key, a hash of the request and the stored response; and ledger entries.", "The deep dives. Idempotency: store the key, the request hash and the response in one transaction. A retry with the same key gets the saved response instead of a new charge. A retry with the same key but a different request is rejected, because that's a client bug.", "The ledger: every movement of money is recorded as two entries, a debit and a credit, that sum to zero. Balances are derived by summing entries, never updated in place. This is double entry bookkeeping, centuries old, and it makes errors detectable because the books must always balance.", "External calls: never call the payment provider inside a database transaction, because the call might take seconds and hold locks. Record the intent first, call the provider, then update the status from the webhook or by polling.", "Reconciliation: every day, compare your ledger with the provider's reports and bank statements. Any mismatch goes to a queue for humans.", "Trade-offs: authorise synchronously while the user waits, but capture and settle asynchronously. And tokenise cards, so raw card numbers never touch your systems, which keeps most of your infrastructure out of PCI compliance scope."]], ["video-streaming-platform", ["A video platform has two completely separate paths: a slow, asynchronous upload and transcode pipeline, and a fast playback path served almost entirely by CDNs.", "Requirements: upload large videos, start playback quickly with no buffering on varied networks, count views, and recommend videos. Uploads are rare compared with views, a thousand to one or more, and the audience is global.", "Estimates: an hour of video at about five megabits per second is about two and a quarter gigabytes per rendition. With a ladder of about six renditions at different qualities, storage is two to three times the raw upload. And egress, the bandwidth to viewers, dominates cost, which makes the CDN hit ratio the key business metric.", "The pipeline: the client uploads in resumable chunks directly to object storage. That triggers a transcode job on a queue. The video is split into chunks so many workers can transcode in parallel into several resolutions and bitrates, each segmented into two to six second pieces, with HLS or DASH manifest files that list them. Then the video is marked ready, and thumbnails and captions run as separate jobs.", "The deep dives. Adaptive bitrate: the player measures bandwidth and picks the quality for each segment independently, so if your connection drops, the next few seconds come in lower quality instead of buffering.", "The CDN: popular videos are pushed to edges ahead of time, and long tail videos are fetched from the origin through a regional shield.", "View counts: stream view events into aggregations, showing approximate counts in real time and exact counts from batch.", "And access control: short lived signed URLs or tokens, plus DRM for premium content."]], ["notification-system", ["A notification system turns events into messages across push, email, SMS and in app channels, respecting user preferences, without duplicates or floods.", "Requirements: multiple channels, preferences and quiet hours, templates in many languages, and delivery tracking. It must handle bursts of millions, like breaking news or a marketing campaign, with at least once delivery but no visible duplicates.", "The flow: services emit events onto an event bus. The notification service checks preferences, renders templates, deduplicates, applies rate limits, and puts messages on per channel queues. Workers send them through providers like Apple and Google push services, an email provider and an SMS provider, and delivery receipts are logged.", "The deep dives. Preferences first: check opt outs, channel choice and quiet hours before queueing anything. Deduplication: an idempotency key per user and event, remembered for a while, so a retried event doesn't notify twice. Per channel queues: if the SMS provider is slow, email shouldn't wait; retry with backoff, and dead letter after a few attempts. And rate limiting in two directions: per user, so you don't spam anyone, and per provider, to stay under their quotas.", "Trade-offs: campaign fan out to millions is a batch job, kept separate from real time transactional notifications. Use priority lanes, so a one time password never waits behind a marketing blast. And track opens and clicks to tune frequency, because too many notifications teach users to turn them all off."]]], "ds": [[null, ["This section covers the distributed systems essentials, the concepts every other section quietly relies on."]], ["distributed-systems-core", ["Let's go through the core concepts carefully.", "Consistent hashing places nodes on a ring, using many virtual nodes per machine so load spreads evenly. When a node joins or leaves, only about K over N keys move, where K is the number of keys and N the number of nodes.", "Replication can be leader follower, multi leader, or leaderless with quorums.", "Quorums deserve care. Overlapping read and write quorums mean every read touches at least one replica with the latest write, but you only get strong reads if versions are compared correctly and failures don't create sloppy quorums. Otherwise, plan for stale reads and repair. Cassandra's quorum settings are a good example.", "Sharding: hash for even spread, range for scans, and salt hot keys.", "Consensus with Raft: elect a leader, replicate a log, and commit when a majority has each entry.", "Vector clocks detect concurrent writes, writes where neither saw the other, so the application can resolve the conflict.", "Exactly once is the most misunderstood idea. In practice, exactly once effects come from at least once delivery plus deduplication or idempotency, with state and offsets updated atomically. And the guarantee stops at system boundaries; Kafka can't stop your email provider sending twice.", "The outbox pattern writes the event into an outbox table in the same database transaction as the state change, and a relay publishes it to Kafka. Sagas chain local transactions with compensations. And two phase commit uses a blocking coordinator, so avoid it across services."]], ["messaging-streams", ["Messaging and streams. A Kafka topic is split into partitions, and ordering holds only within a partition. Consumers form groups that share the partitions, offsets track how far each group has read, and in sync replicas provide durability.", "The key design choice: make the partition key your ordering key. If messages for one chat must stay in order, partition by chat ID; for one user, by user ID.", "Queues versus logs: use a queue like SQS or RabbitMQ for tasks, where each message is handled once and then gone. Use a log like Kafka for events that several consumers read, or that you might need to replay.", "Retries with backoff, and a dead letter queue for poison messages, ones that fail every time and would otherwise block the queue forever.", "And processing: Flink for stateful real time aggregates, using windows and watermarks to handle late events, and Spark for batch."]], ["api-edge", ["At the API edge. REST for CRUD operations. gRPC for internal service calls where low latency and typed contracts matter. GraphQL when clients need to shape their own reads. WebSockets for bidirectional traffic like chat; server sent events for one way server to client streams, like LLM tokens; and long polling as a fallback. Cursor pagination beats offset pagination at scale, because offsets get slower the deeper you go and skip or repeat items when data changes. The gateway handles authentication, rate limiting and routing, and load balancers work at layer four or layer seven. And the CDN serves static files and video, with signed URLs for private files."]], ["kafka-partitions-and-consumer-groups", ["This diagram shows Kafka's partitions and consumer groups, which is where most Kafka interview questions come from.", "Producers pick a partition by hashing the key, so the same key always lands in the same partition, preserving its order. Within one consumer group, each partition is assigned to exactly one consumer. That means if you have three partitions and five consumers, two consumers sit idle; partition count caps parallelism. A different group, like analytics, reads the same partitions independently, with its own offsets.", "Sizing: partition count is roughly peak throughput divided by what one consumer can handle. And be careful adding partitions later, because keys get remapped to new partitions, which breaks per key ordering during the transition.", "A hot key means one partition and therefore one consumer doing all the work. Salt the key or split the entity.", "Delivery: commit offsets after processing, which gives at least once delivery, so consumers must be idempotent. Exactly once inside Kafka needs an idempotent producer plus transactions, and any side effects outside Kafka still need deduplication.", "And health: watch consumer lag per partition. Rising lag means you need more consumers or a faster handler."]]], "cons": [[null, ["This is the theory senior interviewers dig into: consistency, consensus and time. I'll keep it focused on what changes a design."]], ["consistency-models-strongest-first", ["Consistency models from strongest to weakest.", "Linearizable: the system behaves as if there's a single copy of the data, and every read sees the latest completed write in real time order. etcd, Spanner and synchronous reads from a single leader give you this.", "Sequential: there's one global order of operations that respects each client's own order, but it may lag real time. ZooKeeper writes are like this.", "Causal: if one event caused another, everyone sees them in that order. A reply never appears before the post it answers. Unrelated events can appear in different orders to different people.", "Read your writes: you always see your own updates, like a profile edit appearing immediately.", "Monotonic reads: you never see time go backwards, achieved by pinning a session to one replica.", "Eventual: if writes stop, replicas eventually converge. DNS, like counters and Dynamo style defaults.", "The practical rule: pick the weakest model your invariant can tolerate, because each step up costs latency or availability. A like counter can be eventual. A bank balance cannot."]], ["cap-and-pacelc", ["CAP says that during a network partition, a system must choose consistency, rejecting some requests, or availability, serving possibly stale data. And partitions aren't optional in real networks, so it really is a choice you make about partition time.", "PACELC extends it to normal operation: if there's a Partition, choose Availability or Consistency; Else, choose Latency or Consistency. Even with no failures at all, strong consistency costs latency, because replicas must coordinate.", "Examples: DynamoDB and Cassandra by default choose availability during partitions and low latency otherwise. Spanner, CockroachDB and etcd choose consistency both times. MongoDB with majority settings chooses consistency, and it's tunable.", "Quorums: with N replicas, write to W and read from R. If R plus W is greater than N, every read set overlaps every write set. Three replicas, writing to two and reading from two, is the classic choice. And you can choose per operation, stronger for payments, weaker for likes."]], ["consensus-and-leadership", ["Consensus is how a group of machines agrees on something, like who the leader is or what the next log entry is, even when some fail.", "Raft elects a leader using numbered terms and randomised timeouts, so that usually only one node starts an election at a time. The leader appends entries to its log and replicates them, and an entry is committed once a majority has it. With two f plus one nodes, the cluster tolerates f failures; three nodes survive one failure, five survive two.", "Paxos and Multi Paxos give the same safety guarantees but are famously hard to explain. ZooKeeper uses a similar protocol called ZAB.", "Leases give a leader authority for a limited time, which only works if clocks don't drift too much.", "Fencing tokens solve a nasty problem. Imagine a leader pauses for a long garbage collection, loses its lease, a new leader is elected, and then the old one wakes up still thinking it's in charge. If storage checks an increasing token on every write and rejects older tokens, the zombie leader's writes are refused.", "Split brain, two leaders at once, is prevented by majority quorums. That's why you never run a two node cluster without a tiebreaker; if they lose contact, neither has a majority.", "And use consensus for metadata, like leadership, configuration and locks, not for every data write, because it's slow."]], ["time-ordering-and-failure-detection", ["Time is surprisingly hard in distributed systems. Physical clocks synced by NTP drift by milliseconds or more, so never order events across machines by wall clock time.", "Lamport clocks give a total order consistent with causality using a simple counter: on each event, increment; on receiving a message, take the maximum of yours and theirs, plus one.", "Vector clocks keep a counter per node, which lets you detect concurrent writes, ones where neither happened before the other. Leaderless stores use them to keep conflicting versions as siblings.", "Hybrid logical clocks combine physical time with a logical counter, as CockroachDB does, giving timestamps that are close to real time and still respect causality.", "Google's TrueTime exposes clock uncertainty as an interval, and Spanner simply waits out that uncertainty before committing, which gives globally consistent ordering at the cost of a few milliseconds.", "For failure detection: heartbeats with timeouts are simple, but tune them to avoid flapping between up and down. Gossip spreads membership information through the cluster in a logarithmic number of rounds. And phi accrual detectors, used by Cassandra, output a suspicion level instead of a binary verdict, adapting to network conditions."]], ["distributed-transactions-choosing-the-tool", ["Choosing a tool for transactions that span machines.", "First, always try a single database transaction, by keeping related data on one shard. It constrains the data model, but nothing else is as simple or as correct.", "Two phase commit: a coordinator asks everyone to prepare, then tells everyone to commit. It's used inside one database system like Spanner. But if the coordinator dies after prepare, participants are stuck holding locks, so it blocks, and it adds latency. Avoid it across services.", "An orchestrated saga: a central workflow runs each step and, if one fails, runs compensations for the earlier steps. Good for multi service business flows like order, payment and shipping. The catch: there's no isolation, so other users can see intermediate states, and you must design compensations carefully.", "A choreographed saga: services react to each other's events with no central coordinator. Fine for a few steps owned by loosely coupled teams, but the overall flow becomes hard to trace.", "The transactional outbox: write the row and the event in one local transaction, and a relay publishes it. Use it whenever a database update must reliably emit an event. The relay adds a little delay, and consumers must deduplicate.", "And try, confirm, cancel: reserve resources first, then confirm or cancel them all. It suits inventory, bookings and payment holds, at the cost of every service implementing three operations."]], ["crdts-and-conflict-resolution", ["When replicas accept writes independently, conflicts are inevitable. How do you resolve them?", "Last write wins is simple: keep the write with the latest timestamp. But it silently drops concurrent updates, which is data loss dressed up as a policy.", "CRDTs, conflict free replicated data types, are designed so that merging always works, in any order, with no coordination. A grow only counter keeps a count per replica and sums them. A PN counter keeps two of those, one for increments and one for decrements. An observed remove set handles adds and removes. And sequence CRDTs handle collaborative text editing.", "Operational transform is the classic Google Docs approach, transforming each edit against concurrent ones, but it needs a central server to order operations.", "And application merge: keep all the conflicting versions as siblings and let domain logic decide. The classic example is Amazon's shopping cart, which merged conflicting carts by taking their union. Occasionally a deleted item came back, but no item a customer added was ever lost."]], ["saga-with-compensation", ["This sequence diagram shows a saga with compensation. The order orchestrator asks payment to charge the customer, with an idempotency key, and payment succeeds. Then it asks inventory to reserve the items, and inventory replies that they're out of stock. So the orchestrator runs the compensation for the step that succeeded: it asks payment to refund. Once refunded, it marks the order failed and notifies the user.", "Two points to say in an interview. Every step needs a compensating action, and compensations must be idempotent too, because they can be retried. And a compensation is a new action, a refund, not an undo; the charge really happened and will appear on the statement."]], ["raft-replicating-one-write", ["And this is Raft replicating a single write. The client sends \"write x equals five\" to the leader. The leader appends it to its own log at term three, index forty two, and sends it to both followers. Follower one acknowledges. Now two of the three nodes have the entry, a majority, so the leader marks index forty two as committed, applies it, and replies OK to the client. Follower two's acknowledgement arrives late, but it doesn't matter. On the next heartbeat, the leader tells the followers the new commit index, and they apply the entry too.", "Notice the client got its answer after just one follower responded. That's why Raft clusters stay fast even when one node is slow."]], ["distributed-locks-done-right", ["Distributed locks are easy to get wrong, so here's how to do them right.", "A database lock, like select for update, an advisory lock, or a unique row that acts as the lock, is simple and correct if you already depend on that database. Often the best choice.", "A single Redis node, using set if not exists with an expiry and a random token, released by a Lua script that checks the token, is fine for efficiency, like preventing duplicate work. But the lock can be lost if Redis fails over before replicating, so don't use it for correctness.", "Redlock acquires the lock on a majority of independent Redis nodes, but it relies on timing assumptions, and experts dispute whether it's safe for correctness critical uses.", "ZooKeeper or etcd use lease backed ephemeral nodes, where the node with the lowest sequence number holds the lock. They're consensus backed and safe, but heavier to operate.", "Whatever you choose, use fencing tokens: the lock service hands out an increasing number, and storage rejects any write with an older one. Because leases expire, and a long garbage collection pause or network delay can outlast them. Never assume you still hold a lock without fencing.", "And best of all, avoid locks where you can. Single writer partitions, conditional writes that check a version, and idempotent operations often remove the need for a lock entirely."]]], "resil": [[null, ["These are the patterns that turn a box diagram into something that survives load spikes, failing dependencies and bad deploys. Interviewers often move here in the last twenty minutes, so having crisp answers ready really pays off."]], ["rate-limiting-algorithms", ["Rate limiting algorithms, and the trade-off of each.", "A fixed window counter counts requests per time bucket, say per minute. It's tiny and simple, but it allows a double burst at the boundary: a client can send its full quota at the end of one minute and again at the start of the next.", "A sliding log stores the timestamp of every request and counts those in the last minute. It's exact, but it costs memory per request.", "A sliding window counter approximates the sliding log using two counters, the current and previous windows, weighting the previous one by how much of it still overlaps. It's accurate enough and cheap, which makes it a great default.", "The token bucket refills tokens at a steady rate up to a capacity, and each request spends one. It allows short bursts up to the capacity while enforcing the average rate. It's the most common choice, with two parameters to tune: rate and burst size.", "The leaky bucket queues requests and drains them at a fixed rate, giving perfectly smooth output at the cost of queueing delay.", "For a distributed limiter, keep the counters in Redis and do the check and update in one Lua script, so it's atomic. Return status four twenty nine, too many requests, with a retry after header telling the client when to try again. And decide in advance whether to fail open or fail closed if Redis is unavailable."]], ["resilience-patterns", ["The resilience patterns, and why each exists.", "Timeouts: never wait forever. And budget them across the whole call chain; if the user facing request has a one second budget, the third service down can't have a two second timeout.", "Retries with exponential backoff and jitter: they recover from transient errors, but naive retries create thundering herds, so wait longer each time, add randomness, and cap the number of attempts.", "Circuit breakers: after enough failures, stop calling the dependency and fail fast, giving it time to recover.", "Bulkheads: separate thread or connection pools per dependency, named after ship compartments, so one slow dependency can't exhaust every thread.", "Load shedding: when saturated, drop low priority work deliberately rather than slowing down everything.", "Back pressure: bounded queues that make producers slow down instead of letting memory grow until something crashes.", "Graceful degradation: serve cached or partial results. Hide recommendations, but keep checkout working.", "Hedged requests: if a request hasn't returned by the ninety fifth percentile latency, send a duplicate to another replica and use whichever answers first. It cuts tail latency for a small amount of extra load.", "And idempotency keys, which make retries safe for writes. Every other pattern on this list depends on retries being safe."]], ["microservice-architecture-patterns", ["Microservice architecture patterns. Database per service: each service owns its data and shares it only through APIs and events, which enables independent deploys. An API gateway, or a backend for each frontend, gives each type of client one entry point. A service mesh with sidecars adds mutual TLS, retries and telemetry without changing application code.", "CQRS, command query responsibility segregation, separates the write model from read optimised views, so each can be shaped for its job. Event sourcing stores the sequence of events rather than current state, and derives state by replaying them, which gives a perfect audit trail and lets you ask what the state was at any past moment.", "The strangler fig pattern replaces a monolith gradually: put a router in front, and move one route at a time to new services until the old system can be switched off. An anti corruption layer translates a legacy model at the boundary, so its quirks don't leak into new code. And cell based architecture runs independent copies of the whole stack, each serving a slice of users, so any failure only affects one cell, limiting the blast radius."]], ["deployment-and-release", ["Deployment strategies. Rolling deploys replace instances a few at a time, needing no extra capacity, but old and new versions run side by side during the rollout, so they must be compatible. Blue green runs two full environments and switches traffic at once, giving instant switch and instant rollback, at double the capacity. Canary releases send a small percentage of traffic to the new version first, compare its metrics with the old one, and then ramp up. Feature flags separate deploying code from releasing a feature, and give you a kill switch. Shadow or dark launches mirror real traffic to the new path without affecting users, which is great for testing a rewrite.", "And expand and contract for schema migrations, the safe way to change a live database. Add the new column. Write to both old and new. Backfill old rows. Switch reads to the new column. And finally drop the old one. At every step, both the previous and the current version of the code still work."]], ["multi-region-and-disaster-recovery", ["Disaster recovery starts with two definitions. RTO, the recovery time objective, is how long until service is back. RPO, the recovery point objective, is how much data you can afford to lose, measured in time.", "Backup and restore is cheapest, with hours for both. And test your restores regularly, because an untested backup is a hope, not a backup.", "Pilot light keeps data replicated to another region with compute scaled to zero, recovering in tens of minutes, losing minutes of data.", "Warm standby runs a scaled down copy that's always on, recovering in minutes and losing seconds.", "Active passive keeps a full standby and fails over through DNS or a global load balancer in minutes, with near zero data loss if replication is synchronous, or seconds if asynchronous.", "Active active serves traffic from every region at once, with near zero for both, at the highest cost, and with hard problems: write conflicts, usually solved by giving each user a home region, and data residency rules.", "State the RTO and RPO before picking a setup. \"The business can tolerate fifteen minutes of downtime and one minute of data loss, so warm standby with asynchronous replication is enough\" is a much stronger answer than \"we'll go active active\"."]], ["circuit-breaker-states", ["The circuit breaker has three states, named after an electrical breaker. Closed means calls pass through normally while failures are counted. When the failure rate crosses a threshold, it trips open, and every call fails immediately without touching the dependency. That protects both sides: your threads don't pile up waiting, and the struggling dependency gets breathing room. After a cool down, it moves to half open and lets a few probe calls through. If they succeed, it closes again. If they fail, it goes back to open for another cool down."]]], "pds": [[null, ["These small structures make large systems cheap. Each trades a little accuracy for a huge saving in memory or time, and each maps to recurring problems."]], ["structure-use-numbers", ["Let's go through them.", "A Bloom filter answers \"definitely not in the set\" or \"maybe in the set\". About ten bits per item gives roughly a one percent false positive rate, and you can't delete. It's used for a crawler's seen URLs, the read path of LSM trees, and checking whether a username might be taken.", "Counting Bloom filters and cuckoo filters support deletes, at a little more space. Good for cache admission and deduplication with expiry.", "HyperLogLog counts distinct items. With about twelve kilobytes it estimates cardinality to under one percent error, whether there are a thousand items or a billion. Unique visitors is the classic use.", "A count min sketch estimates how often each item appears, using a small grid of counters. It only ever overestimates, never under. Used for heavy hitters, trending topics and top K.", "T digest and KLL sketches estimate percentiles over streams, and are especially accurate at the tails, which is exactly where p ninety nine latency lives.", "A skip list is a sorted structure with logarithmic operations using random levels instead of rebalancing. It's what powers Redis sorted sets and therefore leaderboards.", "A Merkle tree is a tree of hashes that finds which ranges differ between two replicas with a logarithmic number of comparisons. Used for anti entropy in Cassandra, and in Git and blockchains.", "A consistent hash ring decides which node owns a key, moving only about K over N keys on resize.", "Geohash, quadtrees, S2 and H3 answer \"what is near this point\", trading cell size against precision.", "A trie answers prefix lookups, and storing the top K completions at each node makes it fast for typeahead.", "An inverted index maps terms to compressed lists of documents, the heart of search engines.", "A timing wheel manages millions of timers in constant time by bucketing them by tick, used in schedulers, TTL expiry and Kafka's delayed operations.", "A ring buffer is a fixed size queue that overwrites the oldest entry, used for logging, metrics and the famous LMAX disruptor.", "And reservoir sampling keeps a uniform random sample of a stream of unknown length using fixed memory, handy for log sampling."]]], "api": [[null, ["How clients and services talk, and the conventions that prevent incidents."]], ["protocol-picker", ["The protocol picker. REST over HTTP is request and response around resources; use it for public CRUD APIs, and it's friendly to HTTP caching. gRPC uses HTTP two and protocol buffers for typed RPC and streaming; use it for internal, low latency service calls. GraphQL lets clients ask for exactly the shape of data they need; great with many different clients, but watch for N plus one queries on the server and harder caching. WebSockets are full duplex and persistent, for chat, multiplayer games and collaborative editing. Server sent events stream from server to client over plain HTTP, perfect for LLM tokens, live feeds and notifications, and simpler than WebSockets when you only need one direction. Long polling holds a request open until there's data, a fallback when sockets are blocked. Webhooks are server to server callbacks, used for payment status and integrations; sign them so receivers can verify them, and retry them. HTTP three runs over QUIC on UDP, avoiding head of line blocking, which helps mobile users on lossy networks. And MQTT is a lightweight publish subscribe protocol for IoT devices."]], ["api-design-checklist", ["The API design checklist, the conventions that save you from production incidents.", "Use nouns for resources and HTTP verbs for actions: post to orders to create one, get orders slash an ID to read it.", "Idempotency: get, put and delete are idempotent by definition, but post isn't, so accept an idempotency key header on posts that create things like payments or orders.", "Use cursor pagination at scale, an \"after this item\" token plus a limit, rather than offsets.", "Version your API, in the URL or a header, and never break existing clients.", "Use one consistent error body: a code, a message, details and a request ID for support.", "Use status codes correctly: four hundred for bad input, four oh one when not authenticated, four oh three when authenticated but not allowed, four oh four, four oh nine for a conflict, four twenty nine when rate limited, and five hundreds for server errors.", "Return rate limit headers and a retry after header.", "For large uploads, give the client a pre signed URL to upload directly to object storage, with resumable multipart uploads.", "For long jobs, return two oh two, accepted, plus a status endpoint or a webhook.", "And use ETags, both for caching and for optimistic concurrency: the client sends \"if match\" with the version it read, and the update is rejected if someone else changed it first."]]], "secops": [[null, ["These are the wrap up topics that separate a complete design from a whiteboard sketch. Even two minutes on security and observability at the end of an interview makes a strong impression."]], ["identity-and-access", ["Identity and access mechanisms. Session cookies keep session state on the server, so revoking a session is easy; you need CSRF protection because browsers send cookies automatically. JWTs are stateless signed tokens, so any server can verify them without a lookup, but they're hard to revoke, so keep them short lived and pair them with refresh tokens. OAuth two is delegated authorisation, letting an app act on your behalf without your password, and apps should use the authorization code flow with PKCE. OpenID Connect adds identity on top of OAuth, with an ID token that says who the user is. Mutual TLS gives services verifiable identities to each other. API keys are for server to server calls; rotate them, scope them, and store only their hashes. And authorisation models: role based, attribute based, or relationship based, like Google's Zanzibar, which powers sharing in Google Drive."]], ["security-checklist", ["The security checklist. TLS everywhere, and encryption at rest using envelope encryption through a key management service, where data keys are themselves encrypted by a master key. Secrets live in a vault, never in code or container images. Validate input; use parameterised queries to prevent SQL injection; encode output to prevent cross site scripting. Use CSRF tokens, and allow lists for server side fetches to prevent server side request forgery, where an attacker tricks your server into calling internal addresses. A web application firewall, rate limits and the CDN absorb denial of service attacks. Least privilege access, and audit logs for sensitive access. For personal data: minimise what you collect, tokenise it, store it in the right region, and build deletion workflows, as GDPR and India's DPDP Act require. And for payments, tokenise cards to stay out of PCI scope."]], ["observability", ["Observability. The four golden signals are latency, traffic, errors and saturation. The RED method, for services, is rate, errors and duration. The USE method, for resources like CPUs and disks, is utilisation, saturation and errors.", "Logs, metrics and traces each have a job: structured logs with a request ID for debugging specific events, metrics for alerting and dashboards, and distributed tracing with OpenTelemetry to see where the time goes in a request that touches ten services.", "An SLI is what you measure, like the fraction of requests under three hundred milliseconds. An SLO is your internal target for it, like ninety nine point nine percent. And an SLA is the contractual promise to customers, usually looser than the SLO, with penalties.", "The error budget is one minus the SLO. At ninety nine point nine percent, you're allowed point one percent of failures. Spend that budget on releases and experiments, and when it's exhausted, freeze risky changes and focus on reliability. It turns the argument between speed and stability into arithmetic.", "And alerting: page humans on user facing symptoms, using burn rate, how fast you're consuming the error budget, not on every possible cause like high CPU on one machine."]], ["oauth-2-0-authorization-code-with-pkce", ["This sequence diagram walks through OAuth with PKCE, which is how mobile and single page apps should log users in.", "The app first creates a random secret called the code verifier, and computes its SHA two fifty six hash, called the code challenge. It sends the user to the authorisation server with the challenge. The user signs in and consents. The authorisation server redirects back to the app with a short lived authorization code. The app then exchanges that code, together with the original verifier, for an access token and a refresh token. The server checks that the verifier hashes to the challenge it saw earlier. Finally, the app calls the API with the access token.", "Why PKCE? On a phone, another malicious app could intercept the redirect and steal the authorization code. But without the verifier, which never left the real app, the stolen code is useless."]], ["multi-tenancy", ["Multi tenancy, serving many customers from one system, has three models.", "Pool: shared everything, with a tenant ID on every row, row level security and per tenant quotas. Cheapest and simplest to operate, ideal for many small tenants.", "Bridge: a shared application with separate schemas or databases per tenant. Data is separated while compute is shared. Moderate cost, though migrations must run per schema. Good for mid size tenants with compliance needs.", "Silo: a dedicated stack or cell per tenant. Full isolation, most expensive, for large or regulated customers.", "Many SaaS companies use all three: pool for the free tier, silo for their biggest enterprise customers.", "Handle noisy neighbours with per tenant rate limits, quotas, separate queues and routing to cells. Enforce the tenant in every query with row level security, and offer per tenant encryption keys for sensitive customers. And make operations tenant aware: logs, metrics, billing, backups, and data export or deletion per tenant."]]], "follow": [[null, ["After the main design, interviewers throw curveballs. A strong answer names the mechanism, the trade-off and a number, in that order. Let's rehearse the common ones."]], ["curveball-answer", ["Traffic grows ten times; what breaks first? Don't just say \"add servers\". Walk the request path and name the first bottleneck. Usually it's database writes, so shard or queue them. Then the cache hit ratio, hot keys and connection pools. Autoscale the stateless tiers, add read replicas and precompute expensive results.", "One key gets a million requests per second, a celebrity or a flash sale. A local in process cache with a short expiry, key splitting with merge on read, request coalescing so a miss triggers only one database read, and the CDN for anything public.", "A whole region goes down. Health checks plus global load balancer or DNS failover, replicated data with a known RPO, idempotent replays of in flight work, and degrading non critical features while capacity is reduced.", "How do you avoid double charging? An idempotency key stored with the result, a payment state machine, reconciliation with the provider, and treating a timeout as unknown, not as failure.", "Exactly once processing? At least once delivery plus an idempotent consumer or a deduplication table. Kafka transactions give exactly once for read, process, write cycles inside Kafka itself.", "Migrate a live database with no downtime? Expand and contract: dual write, backfill, verify with shadow reads, switch reads, stop the old writes, and finally remove the old store.", "Counts are wrong under concurrency, likes or views? Use atomic increments or sharded counters, flush to the database in batches, and HyperLogLog for unique counts.", "The cache and database disagree? Cache aside with delete on write, short expiry times, versioned keys, or invalidation driven by change data capture.", "Queue consumers fall behind? Scale consumers up to the partition count, add partitions, process in batches, move poison messages to a dead letter queue, and apply back pressure to producers.", "The ninety ninth percentile latency is bad but the median is fine? Look for fan out and garbage collection pauses. Use hedged requests, per hop timeouts, cache warmers and smaller payloads.", "A schema change across many services? Make backward compatible changes, like adding optional fields, use a schema registry, and upgrade consumers before producers.", "How would you test this? Load tests at twice peak, chaos experiments that kill nodes and inject latency, game days, and canaries with automatic rollback.", "And what does it cost? Estimate the dominant item, whether storage, egress, GPUs or database operations, and then reduce it with tiered storage, reserved capacity and caching to cut origin load."]]], "last": [[null, ["And finally, the checklist to read just before you walk in. Let me go through it slowly."]], ["hld-last-minute-checklist", ["State the users, the core actions, and the invariant you must preserve. Everything else hangs on these.", "Estimate only the numbers that change the architecture.", "Show the read path and the write path, and mark which boundaries are synchronous and which are asynchronous.", "Explain cache freshness, your shard key, your hot key plan and your consistency model.", "Make retries safe, and treat timeouts as unknown outcomes.", "Cover overload, failing dependencies, regional recovery and graceful degradation.", "Name the SLO, observability, security, rollout and rollback.", "And for every important choice, say three things: the alternative, the reason you didn't choose it, and how your choice fails.", "That's the whole sheet. You've now got the method, the vocabulary, the patterns and the practice problems. Go in, ask good questions, write numbers down, and think out loud. You know this. Good luck."]]]};
